@page "/learn/circuit-auto-pause"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Pause hidden tabs · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Pause hidden tabs</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Staff leave tabs open all day. Why does every hidden one still hold memory on your server?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Interactive Server · about 6 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> the AutoPause package, which pauses a hidden tab's circuit for you, and waits
        until it's safe. Pausing itself isn't new. .NET 10 added <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</code>, and
        <code>[PersistentState]</code> values already came back on resume. But nothing called it unless your own
        script did.
    </p>

    <p>
        An Interactive Server tab keeps a circuit on the server while it's open: its components, their state, a live
        connection. A hidden tab keeps it too, until the tab is closed. Pausing frees it. The tab's
        <code>[PersistentState]</code> values go to the browser, and come back on a new circuit when the tab does.
    </p>
    <p>
        Below is a stock take. It posts its count to a slow warehouse system, which takes 20 seconds. The stock take
        in Fig. 1 pauses itself the .NET 10 way: a script of its own pauses the tab once it has been hidden for 10
        seconds.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The stock take, paused by your own script" Url="demo/auto-pause/stock-take/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: your own pause script" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Press <b>+</b> on a few products. Press <b>Post count</b>, then at once switch to another browser tab for
            about 30 seconds. Back here, the panel says the tab was paused and resumed, and the counts are all there.
            But the post says <b>Not posted</b>, while the line under it says the warehouse has this count once.
            Press <b>Post count</b> again: 20 seconds later it has it twice.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The script pauses in the middle of the post</h2>
    <p>The script only watches the clock. Here's what happened while the tab was hidden:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The 30 seconds the tab was hidden</span>
        <pre class="snip"> 0 s  Post count pressed, tab hidden     <span class="dim">PostAsync starts: 20 s to go</span>
10 s  <span class="err">script calls Blazor.pauseCircuit()</span>  <span class="dim">state saved: Posted = null</span>
      circuit gone
20 s  warehouse takes the count           <span class="err">Posted is set on a circuit that's gone</span>
30 s  tab shown, resumeCircuit()          <span class="err">new circuit: "Not posted"</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>Posted</code> is only set when the click handler returns. The pause saved the state ten seconds before
        that. The post still finished on the server, but its result never reached the resumed tab. Nothing tells the
        user the count went through, so they post it again.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Let AutoPause decide when it's safe</h2>
    <p>
        Add the <code>Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server.AutoPause</code> package, and turn it on for the page.
        Drop your own script.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;ConfigureBrowser Options="autoPause" /&gt;</span>

@@code {
    static readonly BrowserOptions autoPause = new BrowserOptions()
        <span class="hl">.AddAutoPause(o =&gt; o.HiddenDelay = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10));</span>
}</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        That's all for one page: nothing goes in <code>Program.cs</code>, because <code>AddAutoPause()</code> only
        writes settings into the page, and the package's script reads them in the browser. (To turn it on for every
        page instead, you do register it in <code>Program.cs</code>: see <i>Good to know</i>.) When the timer fires, it holds off while the browser
        is still waiting on the server, as it is until a click handler returns. Then it pauses, with the result saved.
        Here's the same stock take with AutoPause.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same stock take, with AutoPause" Url="demo/auto-pause/stock-take"
                Label=".NET 11: AutoPause">
        <Caption>
            Do the same: press <b>+</b> on a few products, press <b>Post count</b>, and switch away for about 30
            seconds. Back here, it says <b>Posted at</b> a time, and the warehouse has it once. The panel says it was
            paused for about 10 seconds, not Fig. 1's 20: AutoPause waited for the post to finish, so it paused at
            about 20 seconds instead of 10.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>
        Here's the script Fig. 1 loads, the page Fig. 2 runs, and the post they share. The highlighting marks
        something different in each: the old pause in Fig. 3, the new code in Fig. 4, and the unchanged post in
        Fig. 5.
    </p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="wwwroot/js/auto-pause-net10.js" Feature="circuit-auto-pause">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's pause, the .NET 10 way. Highlighted: the old pause. 10 seconds after the tab hides, it calls
            <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</code>, whatever is going on.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/CircuitPause/StockTake.razor" Feature="circuit-auto-pause">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's page. Highlighted: the new code, which turns AutoPause on with
            <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>, also new in .NET 11. The count sheet below it is the same one
            Fig. 1 uses. It skips prerendering only to keep the demo simple: neither AutoPause nor
            <code>[PersistentState]</code> needs that.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/CircuitPause/StockPost.razor" Feature="circuit-auto-pause">
        <Caption>
            Highlighted, but not new: a plain click handler that takes 20 seconds. <code>Posted</code> is a
            <code>[PersistentState]</code> value, but it's only set when the handler returns.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Leave it, or pause it yourself</h2>
    <p>We checked both on a .NET 10 build, with a page that holds a count and a note in <code>[PersistentState]</code>:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways to handle a hidden tab in .NET 10, and what the build did</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">// no script</span></pre>
                <span class="why">a hidden tab keeps its circuit</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ the server held the circuit for the whole time the tab was hidden</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">document.addEventListener("visibilitychange", …)
<span class="hl">Blazor.pauseCircuit()</span></pre>
                <span class="why">pause it yourself once the tab has been hidden a while</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ freed the circuit, and the count and note came back on a new one</div>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ paused with a note half-typed: the text that hadn't reached the server was gone after resume</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        The script works, as far as it goes. It pauses whatever the user is doing: a half-typed note, or a click the
        server is still handling. Spotting those was up to you.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>A pause that waits its turn</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the AutoPause package watches the tab for you. When the tab has been hidden long enough, it checks
        that nothing is under way, and checks again every half second until that's so. Then it calls the same
        <code>Blazor.pauseCircuit()</code>, and resumes when the tab comes back. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It waits for typing too.</b> It holds off while the focused text box holds typing that hasn't reached the server, sound is playing, or Picture-in-Picture or a Web Lock is active. That covers the half-typed note the .NET 10 script loses.</li>
        <li><b>Only <code>[PersistentState]</code> survives.</b> A resumed tab gets a new circuit, so components start again and reload their data. The saved state waits in the browser, encrypted.</li>
        <li><b>For every page, register it in <code>Program.cs</code> instead:</b> <code>app.MapRazorComponents&lt;App&gt;().WithBrowserOptions(o =&gt; o.AddAutoPause())</code>, with a 2-minute wait by default.</li>
        <li><b>Pick one way, at least in RC2.</b> This guide runs on .NET 11 RC2, and this may change by the final release. In RC2, once <code>Program.cs</code> calls <code>WithBrowserOptions</code>, a page's <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code> writes into those app-wide options, so one visit to that page changes every page, for everyone. Use <code>WithBrowserOptions</code> for the whole app or <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code> per page, not both. This app only uses <code>&lt;ConfigureBrowser&gt;</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
